## DECISION TREE (Step-by-Step Logic)

### When You Receive a Cursor Rule File

```yaml
STEP 1: Check File Type
  ├─ Filename contains ".core.mdc"? 
  │   ├─ YES → MODULAR RULE (go to STEP 2)
  │   └─ NO → MONOLITHIC RULE (go to STEP 5)
  
STEP 2: Scan for Appendix References
  ├─ Action: Search for pattern "@rule.appendix.*.mdc#section"
  ├─ Found references?
  │   ├─ YES → Extract list of appendix files (go to STEP 3)
  │   └─ NO → False alarm, treat as monolithic (go to STEP 5)

STEP 3: Context Check (Appendix Availability)
  ├─ Action: Search in conversation history or Project Knowledge
  ├─ Are ALL appendix files in context?
  │   ├─ YES → Full functionality available (go to STEP 5)
  │   └─ NO → Inform user about missing files (STEP 4)

STEP 4: Inform User About Missing Dependencies
  ├─ Message: "⚠️ Modular rule. Requires appendix files: [list]"
  ├─ User Options:
  │   ├─ Upload appendix → Go to STEP 5 (full mode)
  │   ├─ "Work without" → Go to STEP 5 (limited mode)
  │   └─ Cancel → Stop
  
STEP 5: Process Rule Content
  ├─ Extract front matter:
  │   └─ description → Use as rule purpose context
  │
  ├─ Watch for syntax patterns:
  │   ├─ `@reference#section` → Check context, resolve or ask (→ STEP 6)
  │   ├─ "AUTO-TRIGGER @rule" → Note checkpoint, remind user when reached
  │   └─ "## #anchor" → Mark as section for cross-ref targets
  │
  ├─ Execute rule methodology
  │
  └─ At each AUTO-TRIGGER point:
      ├─ Pause execution
      ├─ Inform: "⚠️ Checkpoint. In Cursor, this auto-triggers @rule"
      ├─ Ask: "Should I run @rule now?"
      └─ Wait user confirmation

STEP 6: Cross-Reference Resolution (Sub-Flow)
  ├─ Parse `@rule-name#section-anchor`
  ├─ Check: Is rule-name.mdc in context?
  │   ├─ YES → Find "## #section-anchor", extract content
  │   └─ NO → 
  │       ├─ Critical (SSOT)? → Ask user: "Please provide {rule-name}.mdc"
  │       └─ Optional? → Note: "Working without {rule-name}, may lack detail"
```

